{"name":"Baby Fish Mouth","hex":"#f3acb9","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"baby","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very young human, particularly from birth to a couple of years old or until walking is fully mastered.\n\nA very young human, even if not yet born.","A very young human, even if not yet born."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Picked when small and immature (as in baby corn, baby potatoes).","Newest (overall, or in some group or state); most inexperienced."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/baby"},{"term":"fish","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A typically cold-blooded vertebrate animal that lives in water, moving with the help of fins and breathing with gills; any vertebrate that is not a tetrapod.","Any vertebrate, including tetrapods."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A period of time spent fishing.","An instance of seeking something."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fish"},{"term":"mouth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The front opening of a creature through which food is ingested.","The end of a river out of which water flows into a sea or other large body of water; or the end of a tributary out of which water flows into a larger river."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To speak; to utter.","To represent (words or sounds) by making the actions of speech, but silently, without producing sound; to frame."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mouth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fomalhaut","blocks":[{"text":"Fomalhaut is the brightest star in the southern constellation of Piscis Austrinus, the Southern Fish, and one of the brightest stars in the night sky. It has the Bayer designation Alpha Piscis Austrini, which is an alternative form of α Piscis Austrini, and is abbreviated Alpha PsA or α PsA. This is a class A star on the main sequence approximately 25 light-years (7.7 pc) from the Sun as measured by the Hipparcos astrometry satellite. Since 1943, the spectrum of this star has served as one of the stable anchor points by which other stars are classified."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fomalhaut"},{"title":"Infant","blocks":[{"text":"An infant is a very young human being. Baby is an alternative term for infant. The terms may also be used to refer to juvenile beings of other organisms. A newborn is, in mainstream use, an infant who is only hours, days, or weeks old; while in medical contexts, a newborn or neonate is an infant in the first 28 days after birth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/The_newborn_baby_in_men.jpg/330px-The_newborn_baby_in_men.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infant"},{"title":"Fish","blocks":[{"text":"A fish is an aquatic, anamniotic, gill-bearing vertebrate animal with a tough cranium to protect the brain, but lacking limbs with digits. Fish can be grouped into the more basal jawless fish and the more common jawed fish, the latter including all living cartilaginous and bony fish, as well as the extinct placoderms and acanthodians. Tetrapods are descended from fish, so in a break from the long tradition of grouping all fish into a single class (Pisces), modern phylogenetics views fish as a paraphyletic group that includes all vertebrates except tetrapods. To treat fish as a monophyletic group, one would have to classify tetrapods as a clade/subgroup of specialized fish, which would make \"fish\" and \"vertebrates\" synonyms. In English, the plural of \"fish\" is fish when referring to individuals and fishes when referring to species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Balantiocheilos_melanopterus_-_Karlsruhe_Zoo_02_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Balantiocheilos_melanopterus_-_Karlsruhe_Zoo_02_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fish"},{"title":"Mouth","blocks":[{"text":"A mouth, also referred to as the oral, is the body orifice through which many animals ingest food and vocalize. The body cavity immediately behind the mouth opening, known as the oral cavity, is also the first part of the alimentary canal, which leads to the pharynx and the gullet. In tetrapod vertebrates, the mouth is bounded on the outside by the lips and cheeks — thus the oral cavity is also known as the buccal cavity — and contains the tongue on the inside. Except for some groups like birds and lissamphibians, vertebrates usually have teeth in their mouths, although some fish species have pharyngeal teeth instead of oral teeth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Lion_Showing_off_His_Mouth_%2821454262492%29.jpg/330px-Lion_Showing_off_His_Mouth_%2821454262492%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mouth"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/PiscisAustrinusCC.jpg/330px-PiscisAustrinusCC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/PiscisAustrinusCC.jpg/330px-PiscisAustrinusCC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fomalhaut is the brightest star in the constellation of Piscis Austrinus (center).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APiscisAustrinusCC.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Wide-field_view_of_the_sky_around_the_bright_star_Fomalhaut_%28eso1216c%29.jpg/500px-Wide-field_view_of_the_sky_around_the_bright_star_Fomalhaut_%28eso1216c%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Wide-field_view_of_the_sky_around_the_bright_star_Fomalhaut_%28eso1216c%29.jpg/1280px-Wide-field_view_of_the_sky_around_the_bright_star_Fomalhaut_%28eso1216c%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Wide-field_view_of_the_sky_around_the_bright_star_Fomalhaut_%28eso1216c%29.jpg/500px-Wide-field_view_of_the_sky_around_the_bright_star_Fomalhaut_%28eso1216c%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"DSS image of Fomalhaut, field of view 2.7×2.9 degrees. Credit: NASA, ESA, and the Digitized Sky Survey 2. Acknowledgment: Davide De Martin (ESA/Hubble)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWide-field_view_of_the_sky_around_the_bright_star_Fomalhaut_(eso1216c).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/ALMA_observes_a_ring_around_the_bright_star_Fomalhaut.jpg/500px-ALMA_observes_a_ring_around_the_bright_star_Fomalhaut.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/ALMA_observes_a_ring_around_the_bright_star_Fomalhaut.jpg/1280px-ALMA_observes_a_ring_around_the_bright_star_Fomalhaut.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/ALMA_observes_a_ring_around_the_bright_star_Fomalhaut.jpg/500px-ALMA_observes_a_ring_around_the_bright_star_Fomalhaut.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dust ring around Fomalhaut from the Atacama Large Millimeter/submillimeter Array (ALMA)[36]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AALMA_observes_a_ring_around_the_bright_star_Fomalhaut.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Fomalhaut_Dusty_Debris_Disk_%28MIRI_Compass_Image%29.png/500px-Fomalhaut_Dusty_Debris_Disk_%28MIRI_Compass_Image%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Fomalhaut_Dusty_Deb